        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Prepping the Hole
          </h2>

          <p>
            Preparation is dull, it takes longer than the patching, and it is the difference between a repair that lasts years and one that is gone by spring. The goal is simple: a clean, firm, dry-as-possible hole with solid sides for the patch to press against.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by removing everything loose. Chip out crumbling asphalt with a cold chisel or a mattock until you reach material that does not break away when you pry at it. Then sweep and vacuum or blow out the dust and grit. A layer of fine debris at the bottom acts like ball bearings under the patch and stops it bonding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, square the edges if you can. Vertical sidewalls give the patch something to compress against and stop tyres from catching a feathered edge and peeling it up. A masonry blade in an angle grinder or a sharp chisel is enough for a driveway. Squaring off a ragged hole makes it slightly bigger, and that is fine; a neat, larger patch outlasts a small, ragged one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the hole sits in a patch of alligator cracking or the surrounding asphalt is breaking up, the pothole is a symptom rather than the problem. Patching it in isolation will buy a season at best. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">how to fix a crumbling asphalt driveway</a> covers how to deal with the wider area, and the broader <a href="/asphalt-repair/">asphalt repair</a> guide explains when a larger cut-out patch is the smarter job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, deal with standing water. Water-activated patches and polymer-modified mixes such as EZ Street tolerate a wet hole, but even those perform better if you sweep out the worst of the puddle. For traditional solvent-based cold patch, a dry hole is a genuine advantage, so pick a dry day if you have the choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compaction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Compaction Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you remember one thing from this guide, make it this: a cold patch that is not compacted properly will fail no matter what it cost. Every product on this list works by being squeezed into a dense mass that locks its stones together and presses the binder against the sidewalls. Loose material simply gets pushed out by the first car that turns on it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tamp it until it stops moving, then tamp it again.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A properly compacted patch rings under the tamper and does not show a footprint when you stand on it. If you can still press a heel into it, it is not done. Thin lifts, heavy blows and a final pass with a vehicle tyre are what turn loose mix into a permanent repair.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A steel hand tamper is the minimum tool. Use it in overlapping blows across the whole patch, working from the edges toward the centre so the material is pushed into the corners. For anything more than a small hole, finish by driving slowly back and forth over the patch. Lay a sheet of plywood over it first so the tyre spreads the load and the mix does not stick to the tread.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aim to finish the patch slightly higher than the surrounding surface, around a quarter of an inch. Traffic will compact it a little more over the first few weeks, and a patch that starts level ends up as a shallow dish that collects water. Water sitting on a patch is how the next pothole starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            For water-activated products, follow the maker's wetting sequence exactly. They typically want the mix placed, soaked and compacted in a set order, and skipping the water or adding it after compaction leaves the lower layers uncured. With these products especially, do not rush; the chemistry needs both the water and the pressure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Weather, Water and Winter Patching
          </h2>

          <p>
            Potholes appear in late winter and early spring, which is exactly when conditions are worst for patching. Freeze-thaw cycling opens the surface, water gets underneath, and by March the driveway is full of holes while the ground is still cold and wet. That timing is why cold patch exists, and why the weather rating on the bag matters more than it first appears.
          </p>

          <p>
            Traditional solvent-based cold patch stiffens in the cold. It will still go into a hole in winter, but it is harder to work and slower to cure, so it needs even more compaction. Keeping the bag in a warm garage for a day before use makes a noticeable difference to how easily it spreads and compacts. EZ Street is rated for air temperatures from 0 to 100 degrees, and QUIKRETE High Performance is formulated to stay pliable in cold weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-activated patches are the strongest choice for genuinely cold work. Aquaphalt is sold as an all-season product that can be installed in below-freezing temperatures, and because it cures by reaction rather than evaporation, it does not sit soft for weeks waiting for warm weather. If you are fixing a hole in January that you drive over twice a day, the premium is easy to justify.
          </p>

          <p>
            Summer brings a different problem. Solvent-based patches can soften on a hot afternoon, especially in the first few weeks, and may track or scuff under a turning tyre. Patch in the cool of the morning if you can, avoid parking directly on a fresh patch in the heat, and do not be alarmed if it feels slightly tacky at first; it will firm up as it cures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standing water is the other variable. Several of these products can go into a wet or even water-filled hole, which is a real advantage, but no patch bonds well to mud. Clear out silt and debris even if you cannot get the hole fully dry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sealing Over a Patch
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing over a patch is a good idea, but timing matters more than most people realise. Solvent-based cold patch needs to finish curing before a sealer goes over it. Seal too early and the solvents still leaving the patch can soften the sealer, leave a blotchy discoloured square, or stop the coating bonding at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow the maker's interval. QUIKRETE, for example, recommends waiting 30 days before sealing over its blacktop patch. If your product does not give a figure, a month in warm weather is a sensible default for solvent-based bags. Water-activated patches generally cure much faster, but check the label rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the job in the right order: patch potholes first, fill cracks second, then seal the whole driveway once both have cured. That sequence means you are sealing a sound surface rather than painting over defects, and it gives the patch a uniform black finish so it no longer stands out as a square of different texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a sealer suited to the rest of the driveway rather than the patch. Our guide to the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a> compares coal-tar-free emulsions, acrylics and the thicker sand-filled formulas, and any of them will cover a properly cured patch. Before you seal, clean the whole surface thoroughly; oil spots in particular will stop a sealer bonding.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the patch sits next to paver borders or a paver walkway, protect those before you start. Asphalt sealer and cold patch residue are difficult to get out of porous concrete pavers, and if some does get on them, our guide to the <a href="/best-paver-cleaner/">best paver cleaner</a> covers what will and will not lift it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="patch-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Patching Stops Making Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pothole patch fixes a hole. It does not fix a failing base, and it cannot rescue a driveway that is breaking up across large areas. Knowing where that line sits saves you from buying ten bags of patch for a problem that needs a different kind of repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            The clearest warning sign is holes that keep coming back in the same place. If a well-compacted patch dishes, cracks around its edges and reopens within a season, water is getting under the asphalt and the base below it is soft. Patching the surface over and over will not change that; the soft area needs to be cut out, the base re-compacted, and the section rebuilt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Widespread alligator cracking is the second sign. Those interlocking cracks mean the asphalt has lost its structure over that whole area, and a pothole within it is just the weakest point giving way first. A larger cut-out patch or a professional repair of the whole section will last far longer than several small cold patches sitting in failing asphalt.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough rule, if damage covers more than about a quarter of the driveway, or the driveway is well past its expected life, get a quote for resurfacing or replacement before spending heavily on patch. Money spent on bags and sealer for a driveway that needs rebuilding is money you will spend again next year.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is an argument against patching. For isolated holes in an otherwise sound driveway, a good cold patch or water-activated repair is exactly the right answer, and it is one of the most cost-effective jobs a homeowner can do. The point is to match the fix to the fault.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping the whole bag in at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single thick lift compacted only from the top stays loose underneath, and that loose layer lets the whole patch sink into a dish within weeks. Place material in lifts of 1 to 2 inches, compact each one hard, and only then add the next layer on top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving loose debris in the hole</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crumbled asphalt, grit and dust at the bottom of a pothole stop the patch bonding and act like ball bearings under traffic. Chip back to firm material, sweep it clean and blow out the fines. It takes ten minutes and doubles the life of the repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Finishing the patch flush or low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every patch compacts a little further under traffic. A patch that starts level with the driveway ends up as a shallow depression that holds water, and standing water is how the next pothole begins. Finish about a quarter of an inch proud of the surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using cold patch in shallow depressions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bagged and pail patches need sidewalls to compress against. Spread thinly across a shallow dip or a crumbling edge, they get pushed out by tyres. Use a water-activated patch rated for use without sidewalls, or cut the area square to create proper edges.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealcoating over a fresh patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent-based cold patch keeps releasing solvent while it cures, and sealer applied too soon can soften, discolour or fail to bond over it. Follow the maker's waiting period, often around 30 days, and seal the whole driveway once patches and crack repairs have cured.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

