          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best asphalt patch for a driveway pothole?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a single hole you want to fix once, a water-activated patch like Aquaphalt 6.0 is the most durable choice we tested. For several medium potholes, a polymer-modified bag such as EZ Street or QUIKRETE High Performance Blacktop Repair gives the best balance of performance and cost. Whichever you choose, compaction matters more than brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cold patch asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among bagged cold mixes, EZ Street was the most forgiving in our testing because its polymer-modified binder grips the sides of the hole and it can go into wet holes. QUIKRETE High Performance is a close second with a better yield per bag. Sakrete U.S. Cold Patch is the budget choice if you compact it thoroughly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep can you fill a pothole with cold patch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Any depth, provided you work in lifts. Place 1 to 2 inches at a time and compact each layer hard before adding the next. For holes deeper than about 4 inches, fill the bottom with compacted gravel first; it gives a firmer base and saves a lot of expensive patch material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is cold patch asphalt permanent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, when it goes into a clean hole with firm sidewalls, is compacted in thin lifts and sits in a driveway with a sound base. Most early failures come from poor compaction or from patching over a failing base. If the same hole keeps reopening, the problem is underneath the asphalt and needs a deeper repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you patch asphalt in the rain or cold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Several products are designed for it. EZ Street and QUIKRETE High Performance can go into wet holes, and Aquaphalt is sold for installation below freezing. Solvent-based bags stiffen in cold weather, so warm them in a garage first. Whatever the product, sweep out standing water and mud before placing the patch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can drive on an asphalt patch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most of these patches accept traffic as soon as they are compacted, and driving slowly over them with plywood on top is part of the compaction process. Water-activated products reach full cure in about 24 hours. Avoid parking on a fresh solvent-based patch in hot weather for the first week, because it can scuff under turning tyres.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal over an asphalt patch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it helps the patch blend in, but wait until it has cured. QUIKRETE recommends 30 days before sealing its blacktop patch, and a month is a sensible default for other solvent-based mixes. Patch holes first, fill cracks second, then seal the whole driveway in one go so the finish is uniform.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
